{{also|Modus Operandi}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*med-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|modus operandī}}, from {{m|la|modus||manner, method}} and {{m|la|operandī||of working}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈməʊ.dʌs ˌɒp.ə.ɹæn.daɪ/}}
* {{a|NZ}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɐʉ.dɘs ˌɔp.ɘ.ɹɛn.dɒe/|/ˈmɐʉ.dɘs ˌɔp.ɘ.ɹɛn.di/ }}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|head=modus operandi|modi operandi|s}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A person or thing's normal mode of operation.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Eoin Colfer|authorlink=Eoin Colfer|title=Artemis Fowl|pages=92-93|passage=Failing that, if contact was absolutely necessary, a series of thumb jabs to the nerve cluster at the base of the neck would be his chosen '''modus operandi'''—quiet as a whisper.}}
# {{lb|en|law enforcement}} A known criminal's established habits and mode of work when committing specific offences, especially fraud, matched with characteristics of an unsolved crime to narrow down (limit to a specific list) or [[profile]] suspects.

